<h3>The Memorial Day flood of 2015</h3>

<p>The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/media/ewx/wxevents/ewx-20150524.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service summary</a> of the May 23&ndash;24, 2015 flood describes how quickly it unfolded. Rain of 12 to 13 inches fell in the Blanco River headwaters over four to six hours. At Wimberley, the river rose from about 5 feet around 9 p.m. to about 41 feet by 1 a.m., climbing roughly 5 feet every 15 minutes for an hour of that time. The NWS issued Flash Flood Emergencies for Wimberley and then San Marcos, and the San Marcos River near San Marcos crested at 43.08 feet early on May 24. The flood was part of FEMA disaster declaration <a href="https://www.fema.gov/disaster/4223" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DR-4223</a>.</p>

<p>Five months later, storms on October 30, 2015 dropped rain at rates of 7 inches or more per hour in parts of the county, according to <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=606070" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI records</a>, flooding the Blanco River, Onion Creek, Plum Creek, and the San Marcos River again. Since then, the city has used federal HUD disaster recovery (CDBG-DR) funds for flood mitigation, including the Blanco River Flood Mitigation Project. For more local events, see our <a href="/storm-history/">San Marcos storm history</a> and <a href="/weather-events/">recent weather events</a> pages.</p>

<h3>Why Hays County floods so fast</h3>

<p>Three local features explain why flooding here is so sudden. First, the Hill Country west of I-35 has thin soils over limestone, so heavy rain runs off quickly instead of soaking in. Second, rivers such as the Blanco gather that runoff from a large upstream area and funnel it through narrow valleys toward Wimberley and San Marcos. Third, rapid growth along the I-35 corridor has added roofs, driveways, and streets that shed water into local creeks. East of the interstate, expansive clay soils drain slowly, so water can pond around slab foundations after a long rain. The result is a county where a creek can leave its banks within an hour and low water crossings close often. If you live upstream, our <a href="/service-areas/wimberley/">Wimberley</a> and <a href="/service-areas/buda/">Buda</a> pages cover local details for those communities.</p>

<h2>Flood damage vs. water damage: which policy pays?</h2>

<p>This is the most important question in almost every flood damage insurance claim in San Marcos. Insurance policies treat "flood" and "water damage" very differently, and the wording of your policy controls. In general terms:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Usually handled by</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>River or creek overflows and water enters the home</td><td>Flood policy (NFIP or private)</td><td>Excluded under standard homeowners policies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Heavy rain causes surface water to pool and flow in through doors or walls</td><td>Flood policy</td><td>Rising surface water is generally treated as flood</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wind or hail opens the roof and rain pours in</td><td>Homeowners or commercial policy</td><td>The opening usually must be caused by a covered peril</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pipe suddenly bursts inside the wall</td><td>Homeowners or commercial policy</td><td>Typically "sudden and accidental" discharge; long-term leaks are often excluded</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sewer or drain backs up into the house</td><td>Depends on endorsement</td><td>Often excluded unless you bought water backup coverage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Groundwater seeps through the slab</td><td>Often excluded</td><td>Seepage is commonly excluded in both types of policy; check wording</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>A single storm can produce more than one of these. For example, wind may damage a roof while rising water enters the first floor. In that case two policies and two adjusters may be involved, and damage needs to be documented and separated carefully so that each insurer pays its share and neither can point at the other. For non-flood water losses, see our <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> page.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>NFIP flood insurance basics</h2>

<p>The City of San Marcos participates in the National Flood Insurance Program, which means property owners in the city can buy NFIP flood policies through an agent. Private flood policies are also available and may have different terms. Here is how NFIP coverage works according to <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/get-insured/buy-a-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FloodSmart.gov</a>:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>NFIP feature</th><th>Homeowner policy</th><th>Commercial policy</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Building coverage limit</td><td>Up to $250,000</td><td>Up to $500,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contents coverage limit</td><td>Up to $100,000</td><td>Up to $500,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deductibles</td><td colspan="2">Building and contents are purchased separately and carry separate deductibles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Waiting period</td><td colspan="2">Typically 30 days after purchase, with limited exceptions</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>The 30-day waiting period</h3>

<p>Because NFIP coverage normally starts 30 days after you buy it, you cannot wait until the Blanco is rising to purchase a policy. FloodSmart lists a few exceptions, including policies bought when making, increasing, extending, or renewing a mortgage, and a one-day wait when a property is newly placed in a high-risk flood zone and the policy is purchased within 12 months of the map change.</p>

<h3>What NFIP does not cover</h3>

<p>FloodSmart lists items NFIP policies do not cover regardless of the cause of flooding, including temporary housing and additional living expenses, landscaping, septic systems, decks, patios, fences, swimming pools, most self-propelled vehicles, currency and valuable papers, and personal property kept in basements. That gap surprises many people, especially those used to homeowners policies that pay additional living expenses.</p>

<h3>Proof of loss and claim deadlines</h3>

<p>Check your policy for the exact deadline that applies to you. FloodSmart's <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/claims-checklist" target="_blank" rel="noopener">claims checklist</a> says to submit all required documentation to support your claim within 60 days of the flood, or within the timeframe your adjuster or insurance provider gives you. If you discover more damage later, you can request an additional payment. If your claim is denied, FloodSmart explains that you can appeal to FEMA within 60 days of the date on the denial letter. NFIP deadlines are strict, so keep copies of everything you submit and note the date.</p>

<h2>The January 17, 2025 San Marcos flood maps</h2>

<p>After a revision process that began in 2016, new FEMA flood maps for San Marcos took effect on <strong>January 17, 2025</strong>, according to the <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/402/FEMA-Flood-Map-Revision-Process" target="_blank" rel="noopener">City of San Marcos</a>. The update covers the Blanco River, the San Marcos River, and portions of Sink, Purgatory, Willow Springs, and Cottonwood Creeks. The city reports that more than 341 acres within San Marcos lie in a Special Flood Hazard Area.</p>

<p>What this means for you:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Look up your property.</strong> Use the city's floodplain GIS map or the <a href="https://msc.fema.gov/portal/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a> to see your current zone.</li>
<li><strong>Your lender may require coverage</strong> if your building is now in a high-risk zone and has a federally backed mortgage.</li>
<li><strong>Outside the zone does not mean safe.</strong> Flash flooding along streets and small creeks happens outside mapped floodplains, as the April 2017 street flooding in San Marcos showed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to document a flood claim</h2>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/organized-claim-documentation-folder.webp" alt="Labeled claim folders for photos, receipts, estimates, and weather data next to a laptop" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Organizing photos, receipts, and estimates from day one makes the proof of loss easier to complete.</figcaption></figure>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe first.</strong> Do not enter a flooded building until it is safe, and be cautious with electricity and gas.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything</strong> before cleanup, including the high-water line on walls, damaged flooring, appliances, and the outside of the building.</li>
<li><strong>Measure the water line</strong> inside and outside. It helps establish the extent of the damage.</li>
<li><strong>Keep samples</strong> of carpet, flooring, and drywall where practical, and list damaged contents with brand, age, and approximate value.</li>
<li><strong>Start drying out.</strong> Remove standing water and wet materials when it is safe, and keep receipts for fans, dehumidifiers, and mitigation work.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> to your flood insurer and, if wind or other damage is involved, to your homeowners insurer too.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> has a printable version.</p>

<h2>What a San Marcos public adjuster can do on a flood claim</h2>

<p>Flood claims involve strict deadlines, a dense policy form, and frequent overlap with other coverage. A licensed <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> works for the policyholder and can help by:</p>

<ul>
<li>Reviewing your flood and property policies to see which coverage applies to each part of the loss.</li>
<li>Documenting the building and contents damage and preparing a detailed estimate.</li>
<li>Helping assemble the proof of loss and supporting documents within the required timeframe.</li>
<li>Separating flood damage from wind or plumbing damage when more than one policy is involved.</li>
<li>Requesting additional payment when damage was missed, or helping you prepare for an appeal of a <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied claim</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>We cannot promise a particular outcome, and we do not give legal advice; for questions about lawsuits or legal deadlines, talk with a Texas attorney. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins, and Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

